text:
The diff changes the year constant from `d * 365.25` (≈31,557,600,000 ms) to `d / 365.25` (≈236,550 ms). The `y` constant is used only in the parse path for year units, so every call parsing a year — `ms('1y')`, `ms('2 yrs')`, `ms('-0.5 year')` — now returns a wildly wrong number of milliseconds. Formatting (`ms(31557600000)` → `'365d'`) is unaffected because it never uses `y`. This is an observable behaviour break, not a refactor.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "proof-test.js", "content": "var assert = require('assert');
var ms = require('./');

describe('proof', function () {
  it('parses year units to 365.25 days in ms', function () {
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1y'), 31557600000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1yr'), 31557600000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('2 years'), 2 * 31557600000);
  });
});
"}, "note": "The change replaces y = d * 365.25 with y = d / 365.25, so parsing year units returns ~236,550 ms instead of 31,557,600,000 ms. The test asserts the correct year conversion, which passes on the original build and fails on the patched one."}
```

